<Base title="Methodology and evidence policy" description="How 117 videos and four public guides inform original acquisition education while retaining provenance and uncertainty." type="Methodology">
 <Crumbs items={[{title:'About',url:'/about/'},{title:'Methodology'}]}/><article class="narrow"><header class="page-header"><p class="eyebrow">How to read this resource</p><h1>Keep the explanation connected to the evidence.</h1><p>This site is an editorial companion to practitioner material, not a verified transaction database or a transcript archive.</p></header><div class="prose">
 <h2>The first source collection</h2><p>The research represents 117 public videos and Shorts from {siteConfig.collections['yusufa-sey'].name}. Fifty-seven had native YouTube captions; the remaining sixty were transcribed locally from downloaded audio. All available text was reviewed. That does not mean every recording supplied useful or complete evidence.</p><p>Four recovered items contain no interpretable substantive evidence, and two more are explicitly incomplete. They remain in the source index, with limitations, rather than disappearing from the coverage count.</p>
 <h2>From research to teaching</h2><p>The research classified and cross-referenced 243 evidence records. This site reorganizes that evidence into a curriculum, concept guides, examples, a glossary and numerical references. It adds original explanation and interpretation across sources. It does not publish full transcripts or turn each video into a substitute written version.</p><p>Sources appear beside teaching material and in expandable further-viewing panels. Video links lead to the original YouTube URLs. No timestamps are invented. The source videos remain the primary reference when a detail matters.</p>
 <h2>Evidence is not all the same</h2><ul><li><strong>Educational explanation:</strong> a concept described by a speaker.</li><li><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> a practical heuristic with a limited context, not a universal market condition.</li><li><strong>Hypothetical example:</strong> numbers assembled to illustrate a mechanism.</li><li><strong>Personal or guest experience:</strong> an event as reported in the source, without independent transaction verification.</li><li><strong>Proposed deal or fundraising target:</strong> something being pursued, not necessarily agreed, funded or completed.</li><li><strong>Opinion or unresolved claim:</strong> a judgment or uncertain assertion that should not be read as an established fact.</li></ul>
 <h2>Machine text has limits</h2><p>Both captions and audio transcriptions can contain errors. The recovered audio transcripts receive more caution. Names, loan terminology, amounts and percentages can be mistranscribed. Low-confidence statements are excluded from authoritative teaching or clearly qualified in the reference material.</p><p>Repeated outro text, music and obvious transcription artifacts were not used as economic evidence. Uncertain names are not silently replaced with plausible real companies. Arithmetic conflicts are noted rather than repaired without explanation.</p>
 <h2>The second source collection</h2><p>Four public <a href="/sources/fund-launch/">Fund Launch educational guides</a> add the fund-manager and lender perspective. Source pages identify their organization, title, URL, type, connected topics and lessons. Internal editorial notes classify selected statements as source-derived, synthesis or cross-source comparison with confidence. No full guide text is shipped in the public build. Market ranges appear separately in the quantitative reference archive with their source family and date caution.</p>
 <h2>Finance-specific boundaries</h2><p>Equity fundraising is not private credit. A bank that makes a cash-flow loan is not automatically a non-bank lender. Seller credit is non-bank funding but does not establish a private-debt fund relationship. A named prospective lender is not a completed financing counterparty.</p><p>The Yusufa collection meaningfully discusses institutional and non-bank credit. It does not establish a named completed private-credit facility or executed unitranche or mezzanine structure. Fund Launch provides broader industry and fund-structure education, not verification of those prospective Yusufa cases. The two collections also emphasize different target markets and use “sponsor” in different financing contexts. <a href="/topics/private-credit/">The private-credit guide explains the distinction.</a></p><p>Fund terms, lending permissions and securities requirements can change and depend on jurisdiction and circumstances. Acquisition Companion gives education, not legal, tax, securities or investment advice; obtain professional advice for a real fund or financing.</p>
 <h2>What the site cannot establish</h2><p>This research does not independently verify reported deals, ownership, fundraising, market conditions, laws, tax outcomes or investment returns. The <a href="/numbers/">Quantitative Reference</a> preserves reported amounts and qualifications; it is not a current pricing database. A specific transaction requires its own evidence and professional analysis.</p><p>Publication order is not inferred from file order. A changed view is described as such only when the source supports the comparison. The absence of a concept in the available text is a limit of this collection, not a claim about what a creator knows.</p>
 <h2>Corrections and expansion</h2><p>New collections can add educators, public resources, books and documented cases without changing the site’s independent identity. Future additions should preserve the same source, classification and uncertainty standards. {siteConfig.contactEmail?<><a href={`mailto:${siteConfig.contactEmail}`}>Send a correction</a> with the original source and the passage at issue.</>:null}</p>
 </div></article>
</Base>
